{ Main source: GM Ben Finegold, Reti Opening lecture, 2023-01-31, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DE3jh6OnD10. Finegold frames 1.Nf3 and 2.c4 as hypermodern play: let Black show the center, then attack it with flank pressure, fianchettoes, and transpositions. }
1. Nf3
{ Start with the flexible move. Finegold stresses that 1.Nf3 opponents are often less rehearsed than against 1.e4 or 1.d4. }
1... d5 2. c4
{ The Reti move-order. If you prefer your weak-file habit 2.d4, use the Catalan/QGD game below. }
2... d4
{ The most testing Reti reply in the lecture: Black grabs space with ...d4 and asks White to prove the flank idea. }
3. b4
{ Finegold gives 3.b4, 3.g3, and 3.e3 as serious choices. This is the sharpest queenside version, close to a reversed Benko idea. }
( 3. g3
{ Quiet lecture choice: fianchetto first, castle, then decide whether to break with e3 or d3. You are attacking the center, not ignoring it. }
3... c5 4. Bg2 Nc6 5. d3
{ Finegold repeatedly points out that White can delay central occupation while the bishops and knights restrain key squares. }
5... e5 6. O-O Nf6 7. e3 Be7 8. exd4
{ The exchange on d4 is fine when it leaves White coordinated and ready for Re1, pressure, and piece play. }
8... cxd4 9. Re1 O-O ) ( 3. e3
{ Simple club-practical route: challenge d4 first, then fianchetto. Use this when you do not want the b4 sharpness. }
3... c5 4. exd4 cxd4 5. d3
{ The move d3 keeps the structure compact and prepares development before choosing a central break. }
5... Nc6 6. g3 e5 7. Bg2 Nf6 8. O-O Be7 ) 3... f6
{ When Black uses ...f6 to prop up ...e5, remember the cost: development slows and kingside dark squares can loosen. }
4. e3 e5 5. c5
{ Do not panic about Black space. White fixes queenside targets and attacks the advanced center from the side. }
5... a5 6. Bb5+ c6 7. Bc4 axb4 8. exd4 e4
{ Black can gain tempi on the knight, but the queen and dark squares become vulnerable if Black overextends. }
9. Ne5 Qxd4 10. Qh5+ g6 11. Nxg6 Qxc4
{ This sample stops after the tactical warning: if Black mixes space grabs with slow development, White should look for direct tactics. }
