[Black "Zukertort transposition: Catalan and QGD"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "Codex from GM video transcripts"]
[Opening "Zukertort transposition: Catalan and QGD"]
[Source "GM Ben Finegold, Reti Opening lecture, 2023-01-31, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DE3jh6OnD10"]

{ This covers the weak-file habit 1.Nf3 d5 2.d4. Finegold warns that 1.Nf3 can transpose: if you add d4 and c4, you must be ready for Catalan, Queen's Gambit, and Slav structures. }
1. Nf3 d5 2. d4
{ Your weak file often chooses 2.d4. That is fine, but now the game can become mainstream Queen's Gambit/Catalan theory. }
2... Nf6 ( 2... Bf5
{ If Black plays an early ...Bf5, keep it simple: c4, e3, develop, and use Qb3 pressure. }
3. c4 e6 4. e3 Nf6 5. Nc3 c6
{ The b7/d5 pressure is the recurring antidote to Black getting the bishop outside the pawn chain for free. }
6. Qb3 ) ( 2... Nc6
{ Against the odd ...Nc6 move-order, play c4 and recover the c4 pawn calmly if Black grabs it. }
3. c4 dxc4 4. e3
{ Finegold's dxc4 theme: White can often take the pawn back in several ways, or leave it if development gives enough play. }
4... e5 5. Bxc4 exd4 6. O-O ) 3. c4 e6 4. g3
{ The Catalan-style fianchetto keeps the Reti spirit: pressure the center from g2 and keep move-order flexibility. }
4... c5 { This is the common ...c5 break from your weak lines. } ( 4... c6
{ Finegold names 2...c6 after a Reti/Catalan move-order as an immediate Slav transposition warning. }
5. Bg2 Be7
{ With ...e6 already played, Black's bishop is inside the pawn chain. Develop and play for central pressure. }
6. O-O O-O 7. Qc2
{ Qc2 is a useful Catalan move from your weak file: it supports e4 ideas and keeps d5 under observation. }
7... b6 8. Rd1 ) 5. cxd5
{ After ...c5, cxd5 is the clean way to clarify the center before developing naturally. }
5... exd5 6. Bg2 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. Nc3
{ Develop before chasing ghosts. White keeps pressure on d5 and c-file play available. }
8... O-O 9. dxc5
{ When Black has an isolated d-pawn structure, taking on c5 can be timed after castling and development. }
9... Bxc5 10. Bg5 Be6 11. Rc1 Be7 12. Nd4
{ The lesson: do not memorize one Catalan move. Understand the structure: g2 bishop, c-file pressure, and d5 restraint. }
