{ Many Zukertort weak lines become King's Indian, Pirc, or Old Indian positions after d6/Nf6/g6/Bg7. Use the flexible 1.Nf3 move-order, but once you play d4+c4+e4, treat it as a full center that must be supported. }
1. Nf3 d6 ( 1... g6
{ The 1...g6 move-order is just another road to the same Modern/KID family. }
2. d4 Bg7 3. c4 d6 4. Nc3 Nd7 5. e4
{ White should not be vague forever: once Black allows it, take the classical center with e4. }
5... e5 6. Be2 Ngf6 7. O-O
{ Castle before choosing whether to push d5, exchange, or prepare kingside restraint. }
7... O-O ) 2. d4 Nf6 ( 2... Nd7
{ This covers your Old Indian/Pirc move-order with early ...Nd7. } 3. c4 Ngf6
4. Nc3 e5 5. e4 Be7
{ When Black builds ...e5, answer with e4 only if you are ready to develop and castle quickly. }
6. Be2 c6 7. O-O O-O
{ Qc2 is a recurring weak-file move: it supports e4/e5 tension and keeps a flexible kingside setup. }
8. Qc2 ) ( 2... Nd7
{ If Black delays ...Nf6, the same rule applies: claim the center, then finish development. }
3. c4 e5 4. e4 Ngf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Be2 O-O
{ Do not spend forever on the opening label. This is an Old Indian center; play it by pawn breaks and piece squares. }
) ( 2... g6 { This covers the ...d6/...g6/...c6 branch from the weak file. } 3.
c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 c6 5. e4
{ The same e4 principle: if Black delays central contact, take the center and then support it. }
5... Nf6 6. Be2 O-O ) 3. c4
{ This is no longer a pure Reti: White has occupied the center with d4, c4, and e4. }
3... g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. e4 O-O
{ Black castles and will hit with ...c5 or ...e5. White must be ready to close, exchange, or support the center. }
( 5... Nbd7
{ A direct match to one of your weak lines: ...Nbd7 and ...e5 before Black chooses ...c6. }
6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O e5 8. Qc2 c6 9. Be3
{ Be3 is fine here after Qc2 because it supports the center and connects the queenside pieces. }
) ( 5... Nbd7
{ If Black plays ...Nbd7 and then ...c5, keep the same answer: close with d5 when the structure calls for it. }
6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O c5 8. d5 ) 6. Be2
{ Be2 is the repeated weak-file choice and a sound Classical KID setup. } 6...
c5 7. d5
{ Against ...c5, d5 is the practical space answer. Do not let Black get a free central break without defining the structure. }
7... e6 8. O-O exd5 9. exd5 Re8 10. h3
{ h3 is a useful waiting and luft move in many KID structures, keeping ...Bg4 off the table before Bf4/Bg5 ideas. }
10... Nbd7 11. Bf4 Ne5 12. Nxe5 dxe5 13. Bg5
{ The takeaway is structural: after the center closes, improve pieces and watch Black's kingside counterplay. }
