{ The weak file has several Dutch move-orders. This is intentionally compact: meet ...f5 with development, dark-square control, and either Bf4/e3/c4 or the fianchetto setup. Do not let the Dutch become a random pawn storm. }
1. Nf3 f5 ( 1... d5
{ Some opponents reach the Dutch by 1...d5 and ...f5. Your Bf4/e3/c4 setup still works. }
2. d4 f5 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Nf6 5. c4
{ Once c4 appears, play against e5 and d5 rather than drifting into London autopilot. }
5... c6 6. Nc3 Be7 ) ( 1... e6
{ Against 1...e6 followed by ...f5, the fianchetto is a low-maintenance answer. }
2. d4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. c4
{ The practical target is the same: finish development, then use c4 to make Black prove the Dutch center. }
) 2. d4
{ Your weak lines often use Bf4. It develops before e3 and fights for e5 without committing the c-pawn yet. }
( 2. g3
{ The fianchetto route is very Reti-compatible: Bg2 directly challenges Black's queenside and central dark squares. }
2... Nf6 3. Bg2 g6 4. O-O Bg7 5. d4
{ Only after castling do you commit d4/c4. This keeps the move-order flexible and avoids early Dutch discomfort. }
5... O-O 6. c4 d6 7. Nc3
{ Now you have a normal Leningrad-Dutch-against-White structure with development completed. }
) 2... Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3
{ Build a clean structure: e3, Bd3, castle, then c4. The goal is not to refute the Dutch in five moves. }
4... b6 5. Bd3 Bb7 6. O-O Be7 7. c4
{ c4 turns the setup back into a serious d4 opening and contests d5/e5. } 7...
O-O 8. Nc3 Ne4 9. Rc1
{ If Black plants a knight on e4, trade or challenge it before launching your own plan. }
9... Nxc3 10. Rxc3 d6 *

