2. d4 Nf6 3. c4 d5 4. Nc3 e6 5. Bg5
{ Bg5 develops with purpose and keeps pressure on d5/e6. } ) ( 1... d5 2. d4
Bf5 { Early ...Bf5 plus ...dxc4 is one of your weak-file leftovers. } 3. c4
dxc4 4. e3
{ Finegold's dxc4 theme again: recover the pawn without drama and use the lead in development. }
4... e6 5. Bxc4 Nf6 6. O-O
{ Castle and play a normal Queen's Gambit type position. } ) ( 1... c5
{ Against 1...c5, keep the Reti flexibility with c4 and Nc3 before choosing d4. }
2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e6 4. g3
{ The fianchetto makes sense because the center can become an isolated-pawn or hanging-pawn structure. }
4... d5 5. cxd5 exd5 6. d4
{ Only now commit d4, after Black has shown the central structure. } ) 2. d4 (
2. d4 d6
{ If 1...c6 is followed by ...d6 instead of ...d5, it is no longer a Slav; take the center and transpose to Modern/Pirc themes. }
3. c4 Nf6 4. Nc3 g6 5. e4
{ Same rule as the KID game: once Black allows e4, claim it and develop fast. }
) 2... d5 3. c4
{ Once you play d4 and c4, treat it as a Slav: develop, pressure b7/d5, and avoid random flank play. }
3... Nf6 4. Nc3 h6 5. e3
{ e3 is simple and reliable when Black has delayed central contact. } 5... Bf5
6. Qb3
